<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "Work emails for your whole pipeline — CSuiteFinder",
      description: "$#{@seat_usd} a month per seat, and every month that seat carries $#{@seat_usd} of lookup credit — around #{@seat_emails} verified work email addresses. Try it for $#{@trial_price}.",
      nav: :teams,
      audience: :sales,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:teams)
    ) %>
<header>
  <h1>Your reps know who to call.<br>They just don't have the email.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Give a name and a company, get a work email that has been checked against the
    live mailbox before you send. One seat is
    <strong>$<%= @seat_usd %> a month</strong> and carries
    $<%= @seat_usd %> of lookup credit every month — about
    <strong><%= @seat_emails %> verified work emails</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="hero-cta">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= @seat_link %>">Get a seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/checkout">Try it first — $<%= @trial_price %></a>
  </div>
  <p class="cta-note">
    No contract, no call, no seat minimum. Cancel any time and the month you have
    paid for runs to its end.
  </p>
</header>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.chat(base_url: @base_url) %>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.sheet(
      rows: @sheet,
      rows_label: @sheet_rows,
      date: @sheet_date,
      visitor: @visitor
    ) %>

<h2 class="section-label">Build a bigger Rolodex, today</h2>

<div class="rolodex-band">
  <%!-- Drawn rather than photographed: eleven cards generated from one arc, so
        the fan stays even, the whole thing recolours itself in dark mode, and
        it costs a few hundred bytes instead of a hero image.

        Each card carries its final angle as --a. The cards render stacked at
        zero and open to --a when the graphic scrolls into view; with no script,
        or with reduced motion asked for, they are simply drawn open. --%>
  <svg class="rolodex" viewBox="46 30 468 300" role="img"
       aria-label="A rolodex with its cards fanned open, each holding a name and a work email.">
    <defs>
      <linearGradient id="rolo-face" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0%" class="rolo-face-top"/>
        <stop offset="100%" class="rolo-face-bottom"/>
      </linearGradient>
      <filter id="rolo-shadow" x="-30%" y="-20%" width="160%" height="150%">
        <feDropShadow dx="0" dy="2" stdDeviation="2.4" flood-opacity="0.16"/>
      </filter>
    </defs>

    <%= for {angle, i} <- Enum.with_index(-60..60//12) do %>
      <g class="rolo-g<%= if i == 5, do: " front" %>"
         transform="rotate(<%= angle %> 280 268)" data-angle="<%= angle %>">
        <g filter="url(#rolo-shadow)">
          <rect x="214" y="62" width="132" height="184" rx="8" class="rolo-card"/>
        </g>
        <path d="M272 62 h16 v9 a8 8 0 0 1 -16 0 z" class="rolo-notch"/>

        <circle cx="237" cy="97" r="11" class="rolo-avatar"/>
        <rect x="256" y="90" width="<%= 56 + rem(i * 11, 24) %>" height="8" rx="4" class="rolo-line strong"/>
        <rect x="256" y="104" width="<%= 42 - rem(i * 7, 14) %>" height="6" rx="3" class="rolo-line"/>

        <line x1="228" y1="126" x2="332" y2="126" class="rolo-rule"/>

        <rect x="228" y="138" width="<%= 74 - rem(i * 13, 26) %>" height="7" rx="3.5" class="rolo-line mono"/>
        <rect x="228" y="153" width="<%= 92 - rem(i * 9, 30) %>" height="7" rx="3.5" class="rolo-line mono"/>

        <%= if i == 5 do %>
          <circle cx="235" cy="180" r="9.5" class="rolo-tick-bg"/>
          <path d="M230.4 180.4l3.4 3.4 6.1-6.7" class="rolo-tick"/>
          <rect x="251" y="175" width="62" height="8" rx="4" class="rolo-line ok"/>
        <% end %>
      </g>
    <% end %>

    <%!-- Spindle and tray last, so the cards sit behind them. --%>
    <path d="M96 258 q184 44 368 0" class="rolo-rail"/>
    <path d="M96 264 q184 44 368 0" class="rolo-rail inner"/>
    <rect x="88" y="244" width="20" height="46" rx="7" class="rolo-post"/>
    <rect x="452" y="244" width="20" height="46" rx="7" class="rolo-post"/>
    <circle cx="280" cy="268" r="11" class="rolo-hub"/>
    <circle cx="280" cy="268" r="4" class="rolo-hub-pin"/>
  </svg>

  <div class="rolodex-copy">
    <p class="rolodex-lead">
      A rep's contact list is the whole job, and it stops growing the moment they
      run out of addresses.
    </p>
    <p class="quiet">
      One seat adds up to <strong><%= @seat_emails %> corporate emails</strong>
      a month — every one of them checked against the live mailbox before it
      reaches your sequence. You bring the names; the addresses stop being the
      part that slows everyone down.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:18px 0 0">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= @seat_link %>">Get a seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">What a seat actually buys</h2>

<div class="numbers">
  <div class="num">
    <span class="n"><%= @seat_emails %></span>
    <span class="k">corporate emails a month</span>
  </div>
  <div class="num">
    <span class="n">Included</span>
    <span class="k">deliverability, enrichment, and more</span>
  </div>
  <div class="num">
    <span class="n">$0</span>
    <span class="k">for a lookup that finds nothing</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p class="quiet">
  That is the whole pitch, and it is arithmetic rather than a claim:
  $<%= @seat_usd %> of lookup credit a month, and a seat spends it on work emails.
  Deliverability checks, company data, job titles and email formats are included
  — they draw nothing from the credit. You are charged for answers, so a name we
  cannot resolve costs you nothing.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label">The objections, answered plainly</h2>

<div class="faq">
  <div class="q">
    <strong>Will these bounce?</strong>
    <span>
      Every address can be checked against the live mailbox before you send, and
      that check is included. Two of the rows in the sheet above came back
      undeliverable — that is the point of showing them. A bounce you catch here
      costs nothing; one you catch after sending costs your sending domain.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>What if my reps won't learn another tool?</strong>
    <span>
      There is nothing to learn. They ask their assistant — ChatGPT, Claude,
      whatever they already use — in plain English, and it does the lookups.
      <a href="/start">One line of setup</a>, once.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>What happens to unused credit?</strong>
    <span>
      It does not roll over. Each month starts at $<%= @seat_usd %> again, which
      is why a seat is priced as a month of capacity rather than a balance.
      Credit you buy outright is separate and never expires.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>What if it doesn't work for my list?</strong>
    <span>
      Spend $<%= @trial_price %> and find out on your own names before you commit
      to anything. Same lookups, same terms — it is a trial of the seat rather
      than a different product.
      <strong>The credit expires after <%= @trial_months %> month</strong>, the
      same way a seat's does.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>Am I locked in?</strong>
    <span>
      No contract and no minimum. Cancel from your account page any time and the
      month you have already paid for runs to its end.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>Who is this for?</strong>
    <span>
      Teams that already know who they want to reach — founders selling into
      named accounts, reps working a territory list, recruiters chasing named
      candidates. It resolves people you can name. It is not a prospect database
      and will not build the list for you.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── pricing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     At the foot of the page on purpose: the nav's Pricing link is a bare
     #pricing fragment, so it scrolls down this page rather than sending a
     salesperson to the developer plan. -->

<h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">Pricing</h2>

<div class="plan">
  <div>
    <span class="price">$<%= @seat_usd %></span><span class="per">per person, per month</span>
  </div>
  <p class="gets">
    Every seat gets nearly <strong><%= @seat_emails %> corporate emails</strong> each month.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <%= for item <- @seat_includes do %>
    <li><%= item %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <ul class="caveats">
    <%= for item <- @seat_caveats do %>
    <li><%= item %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= @seat_link %>">Get a seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</a>
  </p>
  <p class="cta-note" style="margin:14px 0 0">
    Rather try it on your own list first?
    <a href="/checkout">Try it for $<%= @trial_price %> →</a>
  </p>
</div>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer(:sales) %>

</div>

<script>
// The rolodex fans open when it scrolls into view, then tilts a few degrees as
// the page moves past it.
//
// The cards are positioned by the SVG `transform` attribute rather than by CSS.
// A CSS transform on an SVG <g> is ignored by some engines — the cards simply
// do not move — so the animation writes the attribute, which works everywhere.
//
// The markup is drawn open. `armed` is added only here, so a browser that never
// runs this, or one whose owner has asked for less motion, gets the finished
// picture instead of a stack that never opens.
(function () {
  "use strict";

  var svg = document.querySelector(".rolodex");
  if (!svg) return;

  var quiet = window.matchMedia &&
              window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
  if (quiet || !window.requestAnimationFrame) return;

  var cards = Array.prototype.slice.call(svg.querySelectorAll(".rolo-g"));
  if (!cards.length) return;

  var middle = (cards.length - 1) / 2;

  cards.forEach(function (card, i) {
    card._angle = parseFloat(card.getAttribute("data-angle")) || 0;
    // Deal outwards from the middle, so it opens like a hand of cards rather
    // than sweeping from one end.
    card._delay = Math.abs(i - middle) * 55;
    card._t = 0;
  });

  var spin = 0;

  function place(card) {
    var deg = card._angle * card._t + spin * card._t;
    card.setAttribute("transform", "rotate(" + deg.toFixed(2) + " 280 268)");
    // Faded in by the same value that swings it, rather than by a CSS
    // transition: a transition is driven by the animation clock and stops with
    // it, which would leave the cards invisible rather than merely still.
    card.setAttribute("opacity", (0.2 + 0.8 * card._t).toFixed(3));
  }

  function draw() { cards.forEach(place); }

  svg.classList.add("armed");
  draw();

  // Visibility is measured in the same scroll loop that drives the tilt rather
  // than with IntersectionObserver. One mechanism instead of two, no reference
  // to keep alive, and it works in any browser that can animate at all — the
  // observer silently never fired in one of the browsers this was tested in,
  // which would have left the cards stacked and the section looking broken.
  var opened = false;

  function visible() {
    var box = svg.getBoundingClientRect();
    if (box.height <= 0) return 0;
    var shown = Math.min(box.bottom, window.innerHeight) - Math.max(box.top, 0);
    return Math.max(0, shown) / box.height;
  }

  function ease(t) { return 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3); }

  function open() {
    if (opened) return;
    opened = true;
    svg.classList.add("open");

    var start = null;
    var span = 620;

    function step(now) {
      if (start === null) start = now;
      var elapsed = now - start;
      var running = false;

      cards.forEach(function (card) {
        var t = (elapsed - card._delay) / span;
        if (t < 0) { t = 0; }
        if (t < 1) { running = true; } else { t = 1; }
        card._t = ease(t);
      });

      draw();
      if (running) { window.requestAnimationFrame(step); }
    }

    window.requestAnimationFrame(step);
  }

  // A few degrees across the scroll, so it reads as an object on a desk rather
  // than a picture of one. The rect is read inside the frame callback, never in
  // the scroll handler, so scrolling cannot force a layout on every event.
  // A few degrees across the scroll, so it reads as an object on a desk rather
  // than a picture of one. Everything is measured inside the frame callback,
  // never in the scroll handler, so scrolling cannot force a layout per event.
  var pending = false;

  function frame() {
    pending = false;

    if (!opened && visible() >= 0.2) { open(); }

    var box = svg.getBoundingClientRect();
    var travel = window.innerHeight + box.height;
    if (travel > 0) {
      var progress = 1 - 2 * ((box.top + box.height) / travel);
      spin = Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, progress)) * 5;
      if (opened) { draw(); }
    }
  }

  function schedule() {
    if (pending) return;
    pending = true;
    window.requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }

  window.addEventListener("scroll", schedule, { passive: true });
  window.addEventListener("resize", schedule, { passive: true });
  schedule();

  // Arming set the cards to opacity 0, so anything that stops the animation
  // from finishing leaves an empty space where the graphic should be. Invisible
  // is a far worse failure than unanimated, and requestAnimationFrame does not
  // run at all in a background tab — which is exactly where this was first
  // tested. So: after two and a half seconds, show the finished fan regardless.
  // Harmless when the animation has already run, since open() only fires once.
  window.setTimeout(function () {
    if (opened) return;
    opened = true;
    svg.classList.add("open");
    spin = 0;
    cards.forEach(function (card) { card._t = 1; });
    draw();
  }, 2500);
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
